<.shell title="Overview" current={:index}>
  <h1>Style guide</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A design system for operational tools: search and rescue, emergency services, and teams
    like them. GOV.UK's patterns and plain look, Carbon's dense tables, in light and dark.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Plain and calm.</strong>
      People use this between calls and after long days. Short words, one primary action per page,
      nothing that moves without a reason.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dense where people scan, roomy where they type.</strong>
      Tables use 32px rows. Forms keep 40px inputs and big targets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Desktop first, phone second.</strong>
      Pages are designed for a laptop. On a phone everything still works and nothing breaks:
      layouts stack, and tables scroll sideways with the useful columns first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard first.</strong>
      Everything you can reach shows the yellow focus, the same in both modes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixed scales.</strong>
      Six type sizes, eight spaces, and a named palette, and classes use the same names: <code>mt-5</code>
      is the fifth space, <code>text-text-muted</code> the muted text token. No value is invented on a
      page; if a design needs a new one, it joins the scale.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tokens, then modes.</strong>
      Components use colour tokens only, so dark mode is a second set of values, not a second
      set of styles.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the examples are made</h2>
  <p>
    Every example is drawn by the app's own components and stylesheet, so it looks as it does
    in the app. A pattern renders the same component as its page, with made-up data.
  </p>

  <h2>What's in it</h2>
  <.table id="guide-groups" rows={tl(groups())}>
    <:col :let={{group, title, _about, _pages}} label="Section" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">
      <.a href={page_path(group)}>{title}</.a>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{_group, _title, about, _pages}} label="What it covers">{about}</:col>
    <:col :let={{_group, _title, _about, pages}} label="Pages" align="right">
      {length(pages)}
    </:col>
  </.table>
  <p>
    Each component's page shows it, says when to use it and when not to, how it works, and
    the code to use it in the app. Pages in each section go A to Z.
  </p>
</.shell>
